## Tests and code style

A pull request that adds or changes behaviour adds or updates the tests for it, and
`just check` passes before it merges. A bug fix comes with a test that failed before the
fix.

{% if stacks | reject('eq', 'docker') | list %}The code follows the style the linters enforce, and `just fmt` applies most of it:

{% if 'python' in stacks %}- Python: [Ruff](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/) for lint and format, ty for types.
{% endif %}{% if 'ts' in stacks %}- TypeScript: [Biome](https://biomejs.dev) for lint and format, `tsc` for types.
{% endif %}{% if 'rust' in stacks %}- Rust: rustfmt and [Clippy](https://doc.rust-lang.org/clippy/).
{% endif %}{% if 'go' in stacks %}- Go: gofmt and [golangci-lint](https://golangci-lint.run).
{% endif %}
{% endif %}{% endif -%}
## Sign off your commits

Every commit in a pull request carries a `Signed-off-by:` line, which says you wrote the
change or have the right to submit it under this project's license: the
[Developer Certificate of Origin 1.1](https://developercertificate.org/). `git commit -s`
adds the line. To add it to commits you already made, run
`git rebase --signoff upstream/main` (the branch you started from) and force-push the branch.

{% if openssf_badge == 'silver' -%}
The `dco` workflow checks every pull request, the maintainers' included, and fails on a commit without the line. Only commits a bot wrote in a pull request a bot opened are skipped. The commit-msg hook refuses an unsigned commit before you push it.
{%- else -%}
The `dco` workflow checks every pull request from a fork and fails on a commit without the line. Pull requests from a branch in this repository skip it: pushing that branch already needed write access.
{%- endif %}

